1
00:00:00.003 --> 00:00:03.673
Welcome to Knowledge Graphs for AI Agent API Discovery,

2
00:00:04.174 --> 00:00:08.078
taught by Pavithra G K, who is head of business knowledge graphs,

3
00:00:08.178 --> 00:00:13.083
and Lars Heling, who is senior knowledge engineer at SAP Business AI.

4
00:00:13.984 --> 00:00:16.186
Large businesses may have thousands of APIs

5
00:00:16.486 --> 00:00:19.089
that have been made available by many different teams

6
00:00:19.489 --> 00:00:21.091
to enable other teams to carry

7
00:00:21.091 --> 00:00:23.593
out many possible tasks across the business.

8
00:00:23.994 --> 00:00:27.397
For example, APIs to help buy or return an item

9
00:00:27.631 --> 00:00:29.299
or request travel approval

10
00:00:29.299 --> 00:00:33.203
or request IT approval for accessing data, and so on.

11
00:00:33.837 --> 00:00:35.172
I think it's great that people are

12
00:00:35.172 --> 00:00:37.107
building so many APIs for AI agents,

13
00:00:37.507 --> 00:00:39.709
but this actually makes it hard for AI agents

14
00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.212
to figure out which APIs to use

15
00:00:42.212 --> 00:00:45.916
and in what sequence to carry out a complex business process.

16
00:00:46.716 --> 00:00:49.119
In this course, you'll learn how to solve this problem

17
00:00:49.119 --> 00:00:50.720
using knowledge graphs.

18
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.058
To help an agent use APIs, you bring raw API specifications

19
00:00:55.058 --> 00:00:56.993
into a knowledge graph.

20
00:00:56.993 --> 00:01:00.464
This gives a structured view of all the APIs.

21
00:01:00.464 --> 00:01:02.766
Additionally, you add information to the graph

22
00:01:02.766 --> 00:01:04.100
to tell it what is the right

23
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.736
order in which you should call the APIs.

24
00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:08.905
Take the example of an agent

25
00:01:08.905 --> 00:01:11.408
given the task of purchasing an item.

26
00:01:11.408 --> 00:01:13.343
It might search for and find the

27
00:01:13.343 --> 00:01:16.546
purchase order API as the most relevant API

28
00:01:16.546 --> 00:01:19.049
and maybe want to call that API to place the order.

29
00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:21.251
But here, the agent might be unaware

30
00:01:21.251 --> 00:01:24.988
that in a big company, it first needs to call another API

31
00:01:24.988 --> 00:01:26.990
to create a purchase request,

32
00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.093
which is then to be approved by the IT department

33
00:01:30.093 --> 00:01:32.762
before finally it places the actual order.

34
00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:36.729
This processed information that the steps needed are to

35
00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:39.332
first generate a purchase request, then get IT approval,

36
00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:42.402
and then finally call the purchase order API.

37
00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:44.471
This information may not be available

38
00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:46.539
in the definition of the APIs.

39
00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.408
And without this type of information,

40
00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:51.311
your knowledge graph and your agent's ability to understand

41
00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:54.247
this important business process workflow and execute it

42
00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:57.450
will be incomplete. That's right, Andrew.

43
00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.920
To capture this, you will extend the knowledge graph with process data.

44
00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:04.557
This turns your graph into not just a list of APIs,

45
00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:07.727
but a network of how they are connected in real processes.

46
00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:11.965
The agent is now aware that the purchase request API

47
00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:14.667
needs to be called first before calling the

48
00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.970
API to create an order.

49
00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.339
To give the right APIs to

50
00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:21.641
the agent to perform a given task,

51
00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.544
you will start with a semantic retrieval.

52
00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:28.181
You will embed all the APIs and their specifications,

53
00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:31.384
and use similarity search to find the best matches.

54
00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:36.689
For example, if the agent wants to create
a purchase order, it will retrieve

55
00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:40.426
semantically related APIs
like the purchase order API.

56
00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:44.197
Calling this API directly
will not be the right action,

57
00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:46.432
as the business process might require

58
00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:49.235
the purchase request API to be called
first

59
00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:51.905
for the approval of the purchase
by a manager.

60
00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:56.209
To solve this, you will extend the search on the knowledge graph

61
00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:58.878
to find and include related APIs

62
00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:01.214
that the business process requires.

63
00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:05.285
This will give the agent all the necessary APIs

64
00:03:05.285 --> 00:03:07.487
and the order in which they should be called.

65
00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:10.390
In the last lesson, you will put all of this together

66
00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.127
to build an AI agent that can carry out real tasks

67
00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:16.496
while following the correct process sequence.

68
00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:19.098
Many people have worked to create this course.

69
00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:21.501
I'd like to thank Christoph Meyer,

70
00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:25.405
Felix Sasaki, and Johannes Hoffart from the SAP team.

71
00:03:25.405 --> 00:03:29.609
From DeepLearning.AI, Esmaeil Gargari also contributed to this course.

72
00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:34.214
Businesses have complex and important processes to get work done.

73
00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:38.418
Implementing agentic workflows to carry out these processes

74
00:03:38.418 --> 00:03:39.886
is a huge part of the work

75
00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:41.821
that lies ahead for the AI community.

76
00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:44.824
And getting this right is an important problem.

77
00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:48.728
The first lesson will be an introduction to knowledge graphs,

78
00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:49.762
what they are and how to use

79
00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.432
them to help agents understand and follow

80
00:03:52.432 --> 00:03:54.434
complex multi-step processes.

81
00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:57.804
So, let's go to the next video and get started.
